ARMSX1 — Changelog
==================

Unreleased
----------

Instruction manual
  * Add a searchable offline Instruction Manual near the bottom of the sidebar, with
    topic-based sections for setup, storage, library, all settings categories, controls,
    saves, cheats, achievements, texture/shader packs, updates and troubleshooting.
    Support touch/controller reading, readable wrapping text and return-to-contents navigation.
  * Move persistent how-to/navigation instructions for tab rearranging, button binding,
    hotkey capture, mapping profiles, skin import, shader activation, sound-effect packs
    and API-key setup into the manual. Keep settings'
    what-it-does descriptions, active input prompts, scope/status information and safety warnings.

OpenGL presentation edges
  * Correct the bottom-origin VRAM offset for OpenGL's interleaved display fields.
    The old calculation sampled one texel below the intended image, exposing font/data
    fragments at the BIOS bottom edge. Preserve both fields and valid image rows;
    leave separate-field and progressive scanout unchanged. Apply a verified build-copy
    hook without changing the pinned upstream checkout or cropping the image.
  * Use high-precision texture coordinates and clamp the final sampling footprint to the
    current frame's first/last texel centers, not the full backing allocation. Prevent stray
    edge pixels during scaled BIOS/game output in OpenGL, ANGLE and the Software presenter's
    flipped upload path. Cover plain and shader-filtered output without cropping, zooming or
    changing Vulkan presentation.

RetroAchievements library and testing
  * Show cached achievement availability/progress over grid and shelf covers, including
    PS one styling and recent games. Unplayed matched games show 0/total; distinguish
    hardcore progress/mastery from softcore completion. Keep badges visible with titles
    hidden, without extra network requests or badges on shelf reflections.
  * Add a persistent Enable RetroAchievements switch at the top of the achievements
    screen. Off unloads the active set, stops evaluation/submissions and clears indicators
    without signing out or deleting synced library progress. Re-enabling during testing
    waits for a fresh game launch so test-state memory cannot earn retroactive credit.
    Reject queued game submissions from an earlier tracking generation; requests already
    transmitted cannot be recalled. Hardcore support is unchanged.

Audio synchronization
  * Bound the SwanStation host audio queue relative to the selected startup reserve,
    instead of retaining up to about 1.49 seconds of stale PCM in its backing ring.
    Drop excess old audio after queue overruns with a short cross-fade and preserve
    complete stereo frames, including oversized batches. Keep the existing startup
    crackle protection and user buffer choice; discard muted/paused PCM before resume.
  * Include queue depth, latency bound, trimmed frames, underruns and Android device
    buffer size in session reports to distinguish host backlog from output/recording delay.

Performance overlay persistence
  * Restore the saved OSD mode at app startup and on the normal PS1 launch path (which
    bypasses legacy renderer setup). Render from the saved global/per-game mode rather
    than an in-memory Off default. Preserve Custom flags and explicit game overrides;
    fresh installs still default to Off.

In-game menu navigation
  * Match controller directions to the responsive menu layout. Compact horizontal tabs use
    Left/Right and Down enters their options; wide vertical tabs retain Up/Down and Left.
    Content uses Up/Down in both layouts. D-pad keys and analog/HAT navigation share this
    policy; gameplay mappings are unchanged.

Independent launcher and gameplay orientation
  * Restore Emulation Screen Orientation in Video settings: Device, Landscape, Portrait,
    and Auto-Rotate, with a separate per-game choice and Use global option. Landscape games
    work even with Android locked to portrait; the library keeps its independently selected
    orientation, including landscape. Image rotation remains a separate control.
    Claim gameplay orientation on the normal PS1 launch path, not only legacy renderer setup.

Memory cards and save states
  * Keep Slot 1 and Slot 2 visible even when a game's card path is not yet available.
    Show a minimal status without extra UI instructions; never redirect an unresolved card
    operation to another game's save file.
  * Track each game's actual mounted cards and add a game selector to the memory-card manager.
    Never redirect a selected game's Slot 1 import to the last game booted; unidentified games
    ask for one normal boot first. Preserve a backup of an existing card before import.
  * Automatic/cold state loads check for changed cards and keep the current card instead of
    silently restoring an older card image over imported/in-game saves. Manual loads retain
    their confirmation. Prevent repeated picker requests and cancel queued state mutations
    after timeout or session shutdown rather than applying them after reporting failure.
  * Preserve memory-card import/export destinations across Android picker screen recreation;
    show an error instead of silently ignoring a returned file. Refuse to redirect a pending
    operation if the active card or data folder changes. Existing cards remain session-locked.
  * Restore Save/Load State touch buttons hidden by a retired-core check and add visible
    Save/Load actions immediately after Resume in the quick menu. Controller actions share
    stable IDs, and existing custom menu orders gain the new actions without being reset.

0.3.6
-----

Performance overlay
  * Replace the disconnected Video debug-panel switch with the real Off/Minimal/Full/
    Custom OSD controls, also available in Interface settings. Restore Custom reading
    selection and keep game-specific settings separate from the all-games default.
  * Open quick-menu OSD controls in This game scope when that game already has an
    override, so changing the displayed mode is not silently masked by its Off setting.
    Fresh installs remain Off by default; the overlay is hidden while menus are open.

Touch controls
  * Save changes directly into an explicitly selected touch profile; add Update profile
    to the profile picker. No new-profile/delete workaround is needed. Selected profiles
    no longer get silently replaced by older game-only layouts on resume or rotation.
    Keep portrait/landscape independent, preserve old game-only edits, distinguish shared
    profile saves from game-only saves, and keep the global selection separate.
  * Add optional Compact shoulder touch areas in Pad settings and the touch-layout
    editor. L1/L2/R1/R2 use artwork-fitted rectangular targets rather than the larger
    circular/square areas, including multi-touch, gliding and tap-to-hold controls.
    Button artwork, positions, sizes and existing defaults are unchanged.

SBI files
  * Keep matching SBI subchannel files beside Android document-folder discs after
    staging and renaming, including CUE/CHD launches, playlist discs and disc swaps.
    Preserve numbered _1 through _5 SBI files for embedded multi-disc PBPs.
  * Match sidecar names case-insensitively and reject malformed/incomplete SBI files
    with a preparation error rather than silently handing unusable data to the core.
    Achievement hash-only scans do not need or copy SBI files.

RetroAchievements
  * Open a matched game's achievements directly in the menu without booting it:
    badge/game artwork, full descriptions, points, unlock status, search and filters.
    Details are fetched only on selection and cached per account for offline viewing.
  * Add a searchable, paginated Library games view inside RetroAchievements for
    verified matched games and softcore/hardcore progress, separate from Current game.
    Already-synced results appear without another API/ROM scan, including unplayed games.
  * Manual sync reloads the saved library and current account when needed; readiness
    messages identify a missing API key, account, library or emulator initialization.
  * Preserve sign-in progress and errors across the periodic account refresh, prevent
    duplicate login requests, and report client/network failures instead of silently resetting.
  * Library sync includes unlaunched games without a detected serial and safely prepares
    Android document-folder images to calculate their real PlayStation achievement hash.
  * Match and display web progress by library URI, without guessing identities from
    filenames. Cache hashes per image/revision rather than sharing by serial.
  * Show disc-check progress; distinguish unreadable images from unsupported hashes.
    Reject incomplete/error API pages instead of overwriting earned counts with zero.
  * Failed/incomplete scans no longer suppress automatic retries for a full day;
    automatic syncs do not stage uncached document-folder ROMs.

Cover art
  * Retry temporary image/network failures and reload failed tiles when refreshing
    the library or downloading covers, even when the game list has not changed.
  * Normalise artwork serials and common filename serial/revision tags.
  * Try verified same-title/region pressings and disc-one artwork when a later
    disc has no image, without changing game/save/cheat identities or custom covers.

Online updates
  * Restore update checks for the normal GitHub app; private builds remain isolated
    and Play builds still exclude the updater.
  * Compare stable versions and dated nightly builds separately, and search a
    paginated release history rather than trusting GitHub's first result.
  * Verify downloaded APK size/checksum, package, signing certificate, version code
    and SwanStation engine before handing an update to Android's installer.
  * Show download/install preparation failures instead of silently dismissing them.

Library and controller menus
  * Custom collections can appear as Home tiles and in the in-game Swap Game library.
    Collection folders expand horizontally on Home and can use custom images.
  * Hold A to arrange Home folder tiles with Left/Right, or hold and drag on touch.
    Saved tile positions survive restarting the app; short presses open folders.
  * In-game menu tabs and options can be reordered with controller holds or touch.
    Selection outlines follow the active control, and controller scrolling reaches
    the ends of menus. Discord has its own entry below Screenshot.
  * Swap Game shows the library and search while the current game stays paused.
    Controller hotkeys support single buttons and two-button combinations.
  * Closing Discord restores menu controls, and launching a game preserves the
    library background colour during the transition.

Android performance
  * Fastest-core placement detects the highest CPU tier even when Android gives
    the emulation thread a restricted initial affinity mask. It rechecks placement
    periodically and restores its original priority when that mode is disabled.
  * Mali software drawing skips unused shading and clips scaled triangle spans
    without changing pixel coverage. These drawing optimizations remain Mali-only.
  * Automatic Mali work reports sample briefly at intervals, reducing diagnostic
    overhead while retaining rasterizer details for investigating frame dips.

0.2.6
-----

PS one interface and session fixes
  * Settings > App > Interface style now offers Original ARMSX1 and PS one.
    PS one adds light-grey console panels, circular controls, blue highlights
    and memory-card-style game tiles in grid, list, shelf and recent views.
  * Switching back restores the existing colour theme and background choices.
    Existing installations keep Original ARMSX1 unless PS one is selected.
  * BIOS/startup and resume audio prebuffers before playback to reduce dropouts.
  * Close & Exit finishes after session shutdown instead of returning to the library.
    External launches respect the return-to-launcher preference.

0.1.3
-----

PSP EBOOT (.PBP) games now boot
  * A .pbp listed in the library but refused to start. It was rejected before the disc
    was ever opened, and two bugs in the reader would have stopped it anyway: sectors
    were reported the wrong way round, and the disc was read 150 sectors off. Single
    and multi-disc EBOOTs now boot, with their cover art, serial and per-game settings.
  * A .pbp inside a .zip is picked as the game to launch.

Crashes
  * Fixed the app dying silently when a game was started after another game failed to
    boot. A failed boot left the emulator in a state that corrupted the next launch.
  * Fixed a game closed and reopened quickly starting up and then immediately quitting
    to the library, and a crash in the same window when the previous game was still
    shutting down.

Library
  * A game called "patches" no longer appears out of nowhere. ARMSX1 ships a patch
    archive with its own files, and the library was listing it as a game — so even an
    empty game folder showed one entry. The app's own folders are skipped now.

In-game
  * Restart in the pause menu closes the menu again, so the reboot is visible instead of
    looking like nothing happened.
  * Picking a game while another one is still closing now starts the game you picked
    instead of restarting the previous one.

Diagnostics
  * New: Settings > App > "Generate log file" writes one report — emulator, interpreter
    and renderer logs, crashes, and Android's own crash records with function names —
    to a file you choose, refreshed every time the app starts. Send that file with a bug
    report and the problem can be diagnosed without the device.

0.1.2
-----

Game folders now really use Android's own folder picker
  * Folders added through the system picker are scanned directly through Android's
    DocumentsProvider, so the library fills without "All files access" — including on
    Android 14+ devices (Oppo/Samsung) where even granting it left the library empty.
  * Games in those folders launch through a per-session file descriptor instead of a
    guessed /storage path, so folders on SD cards and USB drives work under scoped
    storage.
  * Games in those folders are identified off the disc itself at scan time, so they show
    their real cover art and keep per-game settings, play-time tracking and
    RetroAchievements identity — .chd, .pbp, cue+bin and multi-disc playlists included.
  * "Download cover art" no longer silently skips games living in picker-added folders,
    and fetches exactly the art the library grid shows.
  * A game that failed to identify once is retried on a later start instead of keeping a
    blank tile forever.
  * "All files access" is now only about the optional custom data folder; the Play build
    does not request it at all.

Rendering
  * Fixed rendering breaking when the emulator surface is recreated (rotation, leaving
    and re-entering a game): surface callbacks carry an ownership token, so a stale
    surface can no longer be presented into.
  * The in-game renderer indicator no longer sticks on "Unknown" when it is read before
    the renderer finishes starting.
  * GPU identification also reads the board properties Android itself reports, which
    matters on MediaTek devices whose GL strings omit the model.

Audio
  * The default audio buffer is one PAL frame (20 ms) instead of 13 ms, matching the
    core's cadence; a hand-edited value that is not on the settings list now falls back
    to the native default instead of 5 ms.

0.1.1
-----

Cover art for .chd games
  * CHD games now show their real cover art. ARMSX1 could not read the disc's serial
    number out of a compressed .chd, and the serial is what cover art is looked up by,
    so most CHD libraries showed blank placeholder tiles. The serial is now read using
    the same disc reader that plays the game, so a .chd identifies itself exactly like
    a .bin or .cue does.
  * The same fix gives CHD games everything else the serial unlocks: RetroAchievements
    identification, play-time tracking, and per-game settings.
  * Use Settings > App > "Download cover art" to fetch art for your whole library at
    once, now that CHDs can be matched.

Note for existing CHD users
  * A CHD that had no serial in its filename used to store its per-game settings under
    that filename. Now that the disc reports its real serial, those settings are stored
    under the serial instead, so per-game settings, cheats, custom names, per-game BIOS
    and the per-game memory card choice go back to their global values once for those
    games. Save states and memory card files are not affected.

0.1.0 — first public release
----------------------------

ARMSX1 is a PlayStation 1 emulator for Android, built on allkern's psxe core with a
completely new Android frontend.

Interface
  * Brand-new interface built in Jetpack Compose — fast, modern, and made for phones
    and handhelds rather than ported from a desktop menu.
  * Library with grid and shelf views, automatic cover art, and folder support.
  * Themes, accent colours and custom backgrounds.
  * Guided first-run setup for BIOS and game folders.
  * On-screen touch layout editor — move, resize and restyle every control.
  * Memory card manager, and an in-app keyboard for text entry.
  * Live language switching — change language without restarting.
  * Detailed on-screen display: FPS, CPU/GPU load, frame timing, DMA, SPU and more.

Graphics
  * Hardware rasteriser with internal resolution upscaling up to 8x.
  * OpenGL ES and Vulkan presentation paths. GLES is the default.
  * PGXP (experimental) to reduce the PlayStation's characteristic polygon wobble.
  * Aspect ratio options including a fully custom mode, plus a widescreen hack.
  * Texture replacement — load your own upscaled textures.
  * Fixed visible seams along polygon edges when upscaling.

Custom GPU drivers
  * Load custom Vulkan drivers (Turnip and friends) through adrenotools, for better
    performance and compatibility on supported devices.

Shaders
  * librashader support — run RetroArch shader presets, including CRT filters.
  * Full shader parameter editing with save and load of your own presets.

RetroAchievements
  * Sign in and earn achievements, with your profile, stats and unlock notifications
    shown in game.

Extras
  * Save states with thumbnails, plus a warning when a save state and memory card
    have drifted out of sync.
  * Cheats (GameShark .cht), per game.
  * Full physical controller support with remappable buttons and analogue sticks.
  * Skip BIOS for faster boots.
  * Fast-forward with its own audio and speed settings.
  * Discord Rich Presence.

Emulation accuracy
  Many games were fixed by getting the hardware details right:
  * Audio now runs on the correct clock — fixes missing or broken sound in Metal Gear
    Solid, including the alert and sentry effects.
  * Corrected the GPU's drawing-offset readback — fixes clipped and cut-off graphics
    in Xenogears battles.
  * Five CD-ROM fixes covering seeking, streaming audio and read timing.
  * Fixed controller and memory card input dropping out on shared-bus reads
    (Spyro and others).
  * Corrected unaligned stores, GPU status and GPU register readbacks, video mode
    reporting, DMA interrupt handling, and SPU register widths.
  * Whole-program optimisation (PGO) for better performance across the board.

Known issues
  * The widescreen hack changes the 3D projection, so some games (notably Crash
    Bandicoot) stop drawing scenery at the edges of the screen. Turn it off if you
    see missing geometry at the left and right edges. It is off by default.

